Vote By Mail & Early Voting		 
	
		 
			

		 				
	 

	 	
 
	     


         Vote by Mail Massachusetts voters now have the option to vote early by mail in all local, state, and federal elections, with no excuse required. State and Federal Elections:  For state and federal elections, the State mails application cards to each registered voter, postage prepaid.  Local Elections:  The state does NOT mail out application cards for town elections. The Town chooses to mail each family an application enclosed with their annual town census. All ballots must be returned to the Town Clerk's Office by the close of the polls on election day. Use the Secretary of State's  Track My Ballot tool  to check the status of your ballot. The tracker will show you the date your ballot was mailed, the date it was returned, and whether your ballot was accepted or rejected. Absentee Ballots A registered voter who will be unable to vote at the polls on Election Day due to: Absence from the voter's city or town during normal polling hours. Physical disability preventing you from going to the polling place. Religious beliefs can apply for an absentee ballot to be mailed or filled out in the office. If none of the above requirements apply to you, you may still apply for a no-excuse Vote by Mail ballot. Absentee Ballot Applications (PDF)  may be submitted in person, by mail, fax, or by email. All ballots must be returned to the Town Clerk's Office by the close of the polls on election day.  If you are in the military or an overseas U.S. citizen, you are still eligible to vote. To see how to apply for a ballot if one of these applies to you, visit the Secretary of State website for more information and  Federal Post Card Applications (FPCA) (PDF) . In-Person Early Voting In West Newbury, In-person early voting is required and held for state elections only. Please refer to the Election home page for the most up to date schedule. 
     

 
